  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(1-\\frac{3}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=3.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=2$ and $x=4$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(2)^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(4) & =\\frac{2}{(4)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(4)^{4}}=\\frac{1}{128}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection.",
